# chronologia — coarse IntCal20 radiocarbon calibration curve
#
# Source: IntCal20 Northern Hemisphere atmospheric 14C calibration curve,
#   Reimer et al. 2020, Radiocarbon 62, doi:10.1017/RDC.2020.41
#   https://intcal.org/curves/intcal20.14c
#   Local copy: ~/AgentWorkspaces/papers/standards/intcal20_northern_hemisphere_reimer_et_al_2020.14c
# Downloaded: 2026-07-21 (see ~/AgentWorkspaces/papers/standards/INDEX.md)
#
# COARSENESS / HONESTY NOTE: this is the full 1-yr IntCal20 curve DECIMATED to
# every 100th cal-BP sample (0..55000 cal BP). It exists to demonstrate that
# 14C BP and cal BP are distinct reckonings bridged by a tabulated, uncertainty-
# bearing conversion. `calibrate_c14` does a nearest-sample lookup on this coarse
# grid, NOT a probabilistic OxCal/Bayesian calibration: no full-resolution
# curve, no measurement error propagation, no highest-posterior-density
# intervals. Use it as a coarse locator on the BP axis, never as a substitute
# for OxCal/CALIB for real radiocarbon work. basis of results is "reconstructed".
#
# The curve maps a calendar age (cal BP, before AD 1950) to the atmospheric
# radiocarbon age a sample of that calendar age would yield, with a 1-sigma
# uncertainty. Columns:
#   cal_bp<TAB>c14_bp<TAB>sigma_14c
# cal_bp: calendar years before AD 1950 (the sampled grid, step 100).
# c14_bp: mean conventional radiocarbon age (14C yr BP) at that calendar age.
# sigma_14c: 1-sigma of the curve's 14C age at that point (14C yr).
#
cal_bp	c14_bp	sigma_14c
